/14 "London, 1914. You are Max Carrados, the blind detective whose other senses have grown extraordinarily acute through forty years of use.#n#nA telegram has arrived. A railway signalman named Hutchins is accused of causing a fatal accident at Knight's Cross Station. He swears the signal showed clear -- but twenty-seven people are dead and the inquiry convenes tomorrow.#n#nSomething is wrong with this case. The sighted investigators have missed what only you can find.#n#nYour friend Louis Carlyle sits opposite, the case papers rustling in his hands. The familiar smell of old books and beeswax fills the room.#n"

/15 "Turns: "

/16 "Carrados's Study"
/17 "Richmond Terrace"
/18 "Motor Car (Richmond)"
/19 "Klondyke Street, Holloway"
/20 "Motor Car (Holloway)"
/21 "Hutchins Kitchen"
/22 "Hampton Court Street"
/23 "Motor Car (Hampton)"
/24 "Mead's Cottage"
/25 "Mead's Workshop"
/26 "Woundy Station Platform"
/27 "Motor Car (Woundy)"
/28 "Knight's Cross Signal Box"
/29 "City Street"
/30 "Motor Car (City)"
/31 "Pendleton's Office"

/32 "Text adventures use simple ACTION-OBJECT commands.#n#nMovement: NORTH/N, SOUTH/S, EAST/E, WEST/W, IN, OUT.#n#nUseful commands: EXAMINE (or X), TAKE, DROP, INVENTORY (I), LOOK (L), READ, FEEL, SMELL, LISTEN, DRIVE, ASK, ACCUSE, SEARCH.#n"

/33 "Darkness"

; Game messages start at 34

/34 "The paper is cheap government stock, rough against your fingertips. The typewriter keys struck hard and fast -- a man in a hurry, or a man afraid. You set the telegram down carefully. 'Someone wanted this crash, Carlyle. A disaster like this does not happen by chance -- it happens by design. And design requires motive. What does this morning's Herald say about Knight's Cross?'"
/35 "Carlyle reads aloud. The accident coverage is substantial -- twenty-seven dead, the inquiry, Hutchins's name prominent. Then the financial pages. Railway stocks fell sharply on the day of the crash and recovered within a week. Someone bought before the fall and sold at the peak. 'Someone who knew this crash was coming,' you say. 'Who have you heard mentioned in connection with unusual railway stock movements, Carlyle?' He is quiet for a moment. 'There is a name. I had not connected it until now.'"
/36 "'Harold Pendleton,' Carlyle says. 'A stockbroker in the City. I heard his name mentioned at my club last week in connection with a remarkably well-timed railway investment. I thought nothing of it at the time. He purchased heavily three days before the crash.' You stand. 'Then we begin with Hutchins. I want to hear the man's story in his own words before I form any further conclusions. Holloway, Carlyle.'"
/37 "Your fingertips move slowly along the wood. The grooves are regular except at one point -- at what your trained touch tells you is four and seven-eighths inches, the wood is worn fractionally deeper. This measurement was checked many times. 'Carlyle -- the glass fragment. Measure it against this.' He takes both. A pause. 'Four and seven-eighths inches, Max.' 'Of course it is.' You are very still. 'Someone stood at this window and verified that measurement until they were certain. This was planned over days. Possibly weeks.'"
/38 "You turn the fragment carefully in your hands. The edges are wrong -- too clean, too regular for a break. Your fingertip follows the scored line, feeling the deliberateness of it. Cut with a tool. Measured. Intentional. 'It is an inside man, Carlyle. Someone with access to this box, knowledge of this lamp, and a very steady hand. The guard on duty that night was Mead.' You pocket the fragment. 'We go to Hampton.'"
/39 "'Green, Mr Carrados.' His voice is quiet but absolutely certain. 'I have driven that route for fifteen years. I know that signal the way I know my own heartbeat. It was green. Clear. I brought her through at full speed because that is what a clear signal means. They do not believe me. Nobody believes me. But it was green.'"
/40 "A long silence. The kettle mutters. Somewhere above, a child moves across the floor. 'Twenty-seven,' he says at last. The word has weight. 'I keep trying to learn their names from the papers. It seems wrong not to know them.' His voice does not break exactly, but it bends. 'I did not do it, Mr Carrados. I know that is what they all say. But I did not do it.'"
/41 "'There was something...' He hesitates -- the hesitation of a man who has been disbelieved once and fears it again. 'The lamp looked different that night. The green was brighter than usual. Too bright, almost. Cleaner. I mentioned it at the inquiry.' His voice hardens slightly. 'They wrote it down and moved on.' You are already very still. 'Brighter than usual. Not dimmer. Brighter.' 'Yes sir.' You stand. 'Carlyle -- the signal box. Now.'"
/42 "'Iver Mead? Good man. Solid. Known him eight years.' A hesitation. 'He was quiet before that shift. More quiet than usual. I asked if he was all right and he said he was. But he was not.' A pause. 'I should have pressed him. I know that now.'"
/43 "'Pendleton?' He tries the name carefully. 'No sir. I do not know that name.' His voice is steady and untroubled. He is telling the truth. But his breathing carries something else -- a residual tension that is not quite this question. There is more here. You will need to find the right way in."
/44 "She speaks before her husband can stop her. 'He is a good man, Mr Carrados. Whatever they say about him.' Then, quieter: 'There was a man who came to the house. Three weeks ago, perhaps four. George was not home. He said he had business with my husband. He waited. He smelled of money -- expensive, very sure of himself. He left no name. George never mentioned anyone calling. I never told him. The man frightened me, though I could not have said why at the time.'"
/45 "'I wasn't there,' she says. Her voice is steady but the steadiness costs her something. 'He came home at two in the morning. I heard the door. I did not go down.' A pause. 'I wish I had gone down.'"
/46 "He is very quiet. 'A man came to see me,' he says at last. 'At work, not here. About three weeks before the accident. He had a proposal. He said that if I were to allow a certain signal to show incorrectly on a certain night, I would be well compensated. He said no one would be hurt.' His breathing changes. 'I told him what he could do with his proposal. He left. I burned his card. I told no one -- I had no proof and thought no one would believe a railway man over a City gentleman. I should have told someone. I know that now.'"
/47 "'Everything was normal.' The words arrive smooth and rehearsed -- too smooth. 'I carried out my duties as per regulations. The signals were operated correctly. I have nothing to add to what I told the inquiry. I am sorry about Hutchins. He is a good man. But I cannot help him.'"
/48 "A change moves through the room -- small but legible. Mead's breathing shifts: a microsecond of held breath, then released too carefully. 'I did not touch the lamp,' he says. 'That is not part of a guard's duties.' His voice is steady. His heartbeat is not. 'He is lying,' you say quietly to Carlyle, in French. 'About the lamp specifically. He knows exactly what happened to that lamp.'"
/49 "'Pendleton?' The name lands in the room differently from the others. Mead's voice is controlled: 'I do not know that name.' But for one unguarded fraction of a second his breath stopped entirely. 'He knows that name,' you say to Carlyle, still in French, just below hearing. 'His body told me.' Carlyle asks again in English. Mead repeats his denial, steadier this time. But the damage is done."
/50 "'The workshop is private.' His voice carries something harder now. He moves -- and by the displacement of air you know he has placed himself between you and the eastern door. 'There is nothing in there relevant to the inquiry. Nothing relevant to anything. I would like you to leave now, if you do not mind.'"
/51 "Carlyle says pleasantly: 'Mr Mead, I should have mentioned at the outset -- I am here in a semi-official capacity, retained by the inquiry board to collect supplementary statements from all personnel on duty that night. It is purely procedural, but it must be done properly, at a table, and it will take some time. Shall we sit?' You hear Mead's calculation -- the pause of a man who cannot refuse a formal statement without appearing guilty. 'Yes,' he says finally. 'I suppose.' The sound of chairs. Mead moving away from the workshop door. You are already moving east."
/52 "Your hands move slowly across the bench surface -- reading the scarred wood, the stains, the small geography of a working life. Then, at the front edge near the left corner, your fingers find something. A slight unevenness in the wood. Not a natural flaw -- too deliberate for that. You press, and feel movement. A catch. Hidden under the lip of the bench, painted over but not quite flush. 'Found you,' you say quietly."
/53 "The catch gives under your thumb with a small, precise click. A drawer slides out from beneath the bench -- not large, perhaps twelve inches by four, running on well-oiled runners. Someone has used this drawer regularly and recently. Inside: paper. Your fingers find a letter on heavy stock. Folded papers -- several. And something rolled in stiff card. You take your time."
/54 "The drawer is already open. The papers are where you left them."
/55 "You hear them before you reach the door -- Carlyle's measured voice putting a careful question about procedures, Mead's answers careful in a different way. You open the door and step into the cottage. Carlyle continues without pause -- a professional reflex. But Mead stops speaking entirely. You cross the room with the documents secure in your inside pocket, and you can hear Mead's breathing change -- short and rapid, the breathing of a man watching something inevitable arrive. 'Thank you for your time, Mr Mead,' you say pleasantly. He says nothing. His silence says everything."
/56 "Carlyle closes his notebook with a decisive snap. 'I think that covers everything we need for now, Mr Mead. The inquiry board will be in touch.' He rises smoothly. As he passes you he murmurs, almost inaudibly: 'His face, Max.' You are already at the door."
/57 "'Mr Carrados. And Mr Carlyle, is it?' His voice is too large for the room -- the voice of a man who has decided that confidence is his last defensible position. 'I was not aware we had an appointment.' 'We had not made one,' you say pleasantly. 'We find appointments give people time to prepare. We prefer conversations to performances.' A fractional pause. Then Pendleton laughs -- too loud, too long. 'Very good. Well. What can I do for you gentlemen?'"
/58 "'A sound investment, nothing more. I study the markets carefully -- that is my profession. I identified a structural weakness in the railway sector and acted accordingly.' His voice is loud, fluent, well-practised. 'Many investors made similar purchases at that time.' 'Did they,' you say. It is not a question."
/59 "'I do not know anyone by that name.' The denial came before you had quite finished saying the name. You file that away precisely. 'Iver Mead,' you say. 'Railway guard. Knight's Cross section. Ten years service.' 'I have told you. I do not know him.' The cologne is very strong. Beneath it, the first faint trace of fear-sweat -- there since before you arrived, but stronger now."
/60 "The silence that follows has shape and weight -- the silence of a man who has just seen something he feared was coming. When Pendleton speaks his social fluency has gone. 'I do not know what you mean.' 'I think you do,' you say. 'The lamp at Knight's Cross signal box. The glass removed and replaced with a piece cut to four and seven-eighths inches. The piece that made a red signal read as clear at distance. Measured with a wooden ruler that is now in my pocket.' Another silence. Then Carlyle steps forward. 'Mr Pendleton, I must insist you account for the timing of your stock purchases in full. I have the inquiry board's authority and I intend to use it.' Pendleton turns toward Carlyle. His attention locks."
/61 "Carlyle steps forward with the particular energy he reserves for moments requiring controlled aggression. 'Mr Pendleton, I must insist you provide a complete accounting of your railway stock transactions for the period in question. I have the inquiry board's formal authority to demand this, and I intend to use it.' Pendleton turns toward the voice. You are already moving."
/62 "You move quietly around the room while Carlyle's argument fills the space between them. A man with a stick, apparently finding somewhere comfortable to stand. Pendleton does not attend to you. Your fingers find the desk. You begin at the top left corner and work methodically. Correspondence, nothing relevant. A pen tray. A blotter. Then, top right, a leather folder. Inside: official paper, an embossed seal of the stock exchange, and here -- typewritten dates you can trace without difficulty. Three days before the accident. You close the folder quietly and slip the document into your inside pocket. 'Mr Pendleton,' you say, interrupting Carlyle. 'I owe you an apology. I told my colleague I expected to find the certificates in the top right of your desk. I was wrong -- they were in a folder. I am usually more precise.'"
/63 "Pendleton stares. 'You searched--' 'Your desk,' you confirm. 'While you were attending to Carlyle. A man with a stick, moving around a room, does not draw the same attention as a man with two working eyes. It is one of the condition's few advantages.' You place the certificate on the desk between you. 'The date of purchase. Three days before the accident you arranged. The letter detailing your instructions to Mead and your threats against his family. The receipts documenting your payments to him. The diagram showing the exact measurement you calculated for the glass. The ruler from the signal box confirming that calculation was executed precisely. And the glass itself.' You stop. 'Twenty-seven people, Mr Pendleton. Shall I go on?' The silence that follows is the longest you have heard in a long career."
/64 "CONGRATULATIONS. You have solved the Knight's Cross Signal Problem. Harold Pendleton will face justice for his crimes, Mead for his reluctant complicity. And Hutchins -- innocent Hutchins -- will keep his job and his freedom. Pendleton does not confess. Men like Pendleton rarely do, not immediately. But he does not deny either. He sits down heavily and says nothing at all, and in that silence his guilt is as plain as anything you have ever read with your hands. The blind detective sees what sighted men missed."
/65 "You need more before you can make this accusation formally. The ruler and glass establish the method. The certificate establishes the motive. You need the evidence from the workshop connecting Pendleton to Mead directly -- the letter, the receipts, the diagram. Without those, Pendleton walks free. 'I will return, Mr Pendleton,' you say pleasantly. 'Please do not go anywhere.'"
/66 "'Not yet, Carlyle. I need to understand what we are walking into before we walk into it. There is more to learn here first.'"
/67 "'The signal box can wait. I want to hear Hutchins tell his story in his own words first. A man's voice tells you things a report cannot. I am not ready for the signal box yet.'"
/68 "'I am not ready to face Mead yet. I need to understand the method before I confront the man who used it. The signal box first.'"
/69 "'I have suspicion, Carlyle, and suspicion is worthless in a court of law. I need what is in that workshop before I face Pendleton. We are not finished at Hampton.'"
/70 "Mead has positioned himself between you and the workshop door. The displacement of air is unmistakeable. He will not allow you through without a reason to look elsewhere. You need Carlyle to give him one."
/71 "Pendleton is watching the desk too carefully. You need Carlyle to occupy his attention before you can search it."
/72 "Parkinson pulls into the traffic and you settle back, the city moving around you in sound and smell. 'Tell me about Hutchins,' you say. Carlyle opens the file. 'Forty-three years old. Started on the railway at sixteen. Fifteen years on the Knight's Cross route without incident. His supervisor describes him as meticulous.' 'And yet,' you say. 'And yet,' Carlyle agrees."
/73 "'Brighter than usual,' Carlyle repeats, as Parkinson navigates south. 'What does that mean precisely, Max?' 'It means someone replaced the red glass in that signal lamp with a piece calculated to allow enough light through to read as clear at distance. Red light through clear glass at the right width produces exactly that effect -- brighter, cleaner, wrong. That requires knowledge of the mechanism. And access to the box.' 'An inside man,' you confirm. 'Let us go and see what he left behind.'"
/74 "The motor car moves through quieter streets. 'Mead had access,' Carlyle says, 'and Mead was frightened before his shift, and Mead's pulse changed when I mentioned a certain name this morning.' 'You suspected before we left the study,' Carlyle says. 'I suspected. Suspicion is not evidence. Evidence is what we are going to find.'"
/75 "You sit in the motor car with the documents in your inside pocket. 'Read me the letter,' you say. Carlyle reads. Pendleton's words, Pendleton's threats. 'He was certain he would never be caught,' you say. 'He did not plan for a blind detective.' 'No,' Carlyle says quietly. 'They rarely do.' 'The City, Parkinson.'"
/76 "The motor car turns back through familiar streets toward Kensington. The city changes around you as you move -- the sound of it softens, the smell shifts from commerce to coal smoke and cooking. But the case is not finished."
/77 "'The bare facts, Max. A collision at Knight's Cross three nights ago. Twenty-seven dead, nineteen injured. Engine-driver Hutchins claims the signal showed clear. The inquiry convenes tomorrow morning. If Hutchins cannot produce an explanation he will be held responsible -- his licence revoked, possible criminal proceedings. He has no explanation. Only his word.'"
/78 "'Fifteen years on that route without incident. Exemplary record. Known to be careful, methodical, not a drinking man. His colleagues speak well of him.' Carlyle's voice carries a note of frustration. 'By every measure he is exactly the kind of man who does not make this kind of mistake. And yet twenty-seven people are dead.'"
/79 "'The 11:42 from Paddington. Hutchins brought it through Knight's Cross at full speed -- the signal showed clear, he says. But the signal was red. The train struck a goods wagon on the main line.' Carlyle stops. 'It was very bad, Max.'"
/80 "'Harold Pendleton. Stockbroker, City of London. Moderately successful until recently -- there were rumours of difficulties. Then three days before the Knight's Cross accident he purchased a significant quantity of railway stock at depressed prices. Sold it a week after the crash at considerable profit.' A pause. 'It may be coincidence.' His tone suggests he does not believe it."
/81 "'Iver Mead. Railway guard, ten years service. The guard on duty at Knight's Cross the night of the accident. He was responsible for the signals in that section. The inquiry has not focused on Mead. Hutchins is the easier target.'"
/82 "'Carlyle,' you say, 'describe what you can.' He clears his throat and begins -- the particular methodical description he uses when he knows you need information rather than impressions."
/83 "There is no one here to be your eyes. But you do not need eyes for this room. You have something better: forty years of learning to read the world through your hands and ears and nose. Stand still for a moment. Listen to what the room has to say."
/84 "Your armchair. Forty years of use have shaped it precisely to you -- the leather worn smooth at the armrests, the cushion settled at the exact angle you prefer. You have solved eleven cases in this chair. You settle back and feel, as you always do, that thought comes more easily here than anywhere else."
/85 "Three walls, floor to ceiling -- you know this from the particular acoustic quality of the room, the way sound behaves. Your fingers find the nearest shelf. Every spine carries a small adhesive marker in the corner, braille-coded in your own system. Yours is organised by usefulness."
/86 "Three electric lamps at full power -- you feel their warmth on your face and hands, a gentle steady radiation. Your housekeeper once asked why a blind man needed so much light. You told her it was for visitors. This is not entirely true."
/87 "A large desk, partners style, inherited from your father. Everything has a specific location known by distance and angle from the near left corner. The telegram is at the front."
/88 "The cobblestones of Richmond Terrace are as familiar as furniture. You know the slightly raised stone three paces from the gate, the place where the kerb drops an inch lower than it should. You could manage it at speed in any weather."
/89 "You rest your hands on the table surface. Scrubbed pine, the grain raised fractionally by years of washing. The edges are worn round by elbows and forearms across two decades. This is a peaceful house. Or was, until three nights ago."
/90 "The kettle mutters on the range with the comfortable sound of something about to boil. Someone put the kettle on when they heard you at the door -- the reflex of a household that receives guests properly even in the middle of a catastrophe. Nobody has yet moved to make the tea."
/91 "You lift one carefully. Bone china -- you can tell by the weight and the faint resonance when you set it back down. The good cups. Brought out for company. This household has not given up."
/92 "You crouch and rest two fingers briefly on the nearest rail. Cold iron, and beneath the cold a faint vibration -- a distant train running somewhere on the network. This particular line has carried a message that twenty-seven people read too late."
/93 "Old timber, deeply impregnated with creosote -- you can smell it with every breath and feel the slight roughness through the soles of your shoes. The platform has its own acoustic quality: sound travels differently here, less enclosed than a building, more directed than open ground."
/94 "The lever frame runs the width of the box -- you count them by touch, moving along the row. Cold iron, each lever in its correct position, the paint worn away by the grip of generations of signalmen. Someone in this box subverted that mechanism. The thought has a quality of cold that has nothing to do with the temperature."
/95 "The block instruments tick and click with bureaucratic precision -- recording the movement of trains, the occupation of sections of track. They were doing this on the night of the accident. The instruments do not lie. Someone took care to ensure that what they recorded was itself a lie."
/96 "You move carefully along the floor near the lamp housing, your stick preceding you. Splinters of glass, small and sharp. And here: two parallel impressions in the accumulated dust where something rested on the floor. A case or bag, set down while someone worked. The marks are precise. This was not improvised."
/97 "A mantel clock -- you can identify the maker by the particular cadence of the tick, a double beat with a slight emphasis on the second. An expensive movement for a railway guard's cottage. The sound of it fills the room with a regularity that is almost confrontational in these circumstances."
/98 "Your fingers find the antimacassar on the nearest chair back. Fresh laundering -- the stiffness of something dried quickly, possibly this morning. Someone cleaned this house thoroughly and recently. People clean their houses when they are expecting something to happen."
/99 "Cold grate. In weather like this the fireplace should have been lit this morning. Your fingers find the ash -- cold, undisturbed. Mead has been in a state of mind today where ordinary domestic warmth did not occur to him. He has been waiting."
/100 "A plain door, heavier than interior doors usually are. When you move toward it, Mead moves too -- the displacement of air is unmistakeable. He has been tracking your position since you arrived and placing himself between you and this door throughout."
/101 "You move slowly across the floor, attending to what your feet tell you. Stone flags at the entrance. Then the surface changes: gritty underfoot, a faint metallic quality -- metal filings embedded in the stone over years of work. Then near the bench, fine wood shavings. And here, near the left corner, a patch where the floor has been scrubbed recently and more thoroughly than the rest."
/102 "The apron hangs on a peg just inside the door. Heavy leather, stiff with dried compounds. Machine oil, predominant. Then something sharper -- chemical, precise, in the family of things used in fine work. And beneath that, almost gone but not quite: the residue of an expensive cologne. You have encountered that cologne before."
/103 "You reach out to the wall. Papers, pinned there -- you can feel the edges of several sheets. Engineering drawings, by the weight and stiffness of the card. You take one down. Embossed lines -- a technical diagram. Precise angles. Dimensions you cannot read. But the nature of the drawing is clear to you, and it confirms what you already know about this room and this man."
/104 "'I can describe what I observe, Mr Carrados.' Carlyle pauses. 'A well-maintained building. Fresh paint on the window surrounds. The garden path is tidy. The curtain at the front window has just moved.' Another pause. 'Someone inside has noted our arrival and chosen not to come to the door. And the side of the building -- a path around the left side leading to a separate structure at the rear. A workshop, by the look of it.' 'A workshop,' you say. 'Almost certainly.'"
/105 "Well tended -- your feet find no weeds on the path, the edges are defined. There is a particular smell to a cared-for garden in winter: cold earth, the ghost of last summer's plants. Today you notice only that the path to the workshop has been recently used. The earth is disturbed."
/106 "Recently used -- the warmth is still faintly perceptible, and the smell of burned paper is strongest here. You crouch and feel the grate. Ash, still slightly warm. Pendleton has been burning documents this morning. He has not been busy enough."
/107 "Open, despite the cold. Someone wanted air circulation, or wanted the smell of burning to dissipate. It has not dissipated. 'He knew we were coming,' you say quietly to Carlyle. 'He has been expecting something ever since the accident.'"
/108 "'Certificates,' Carlyle murmurs. 'Framed. Professional qualifications, memberships of various institutions. A photograph of Pendleton with someone official-looking.' He pauses. 'He needs people to know he is legitimate.' 'Men who are legitimate,' you say, 'rarely need to advertise it quite so thoroughly.'"
/109 "The City comes at you from every direction -- rapid footsteps on stone, the rustle of newspapers, fragments of urgent conversation about yields and margins. Twenty-seven people died in a railway accident three nights ago. Here it has already become a line in a financial report."
/110 "A boy's voice, close and sharp, announcing something about railway stocks and recovery. The word recovery. In your pocket: a letter, three receipts, a diagram. Someone else's idea of a recovery."
/111 "Carlyle has already read you the relevant sections. The railway stocks. The timing. The name."
/112 "A document on heavy parchment, stiff and formal under your fingers. The embossed seal of the stock exchange in the top corner. The typewritten dates leave impressions you can trace: three days before the accident; nine days after. A purchase and a sale, timed with knowledge that could only have come from certainty of what was going to happen."
/113 "Heavy paper, expensive -- you can tell by its weight and the faint crackle when you flex it. An embossed address at the top: something in the City. The typewriter impressions are deep and forceful throughout. The paper carries the residue of cigar smoke and, very faintly, the same cologne you have now encountered twice."
/114 "Three folded papers, each identical in weight and texture. Each bears an embossed seal in the upper corner -- the Bank of England, unmistakeable. Three payments. Three dates. The paper is of the quality associated with substantial transactions."
/115 "Rolled card, stiff. An engineering drawing -- you unroll it and let your fingers read the embossed lines: parallel edges, precise angles, a cross-section of some housing. And in the corner, a single measurement your fingers trace with complete recognition. Four and seven-eighths inches."
/116 "Louis Carlyle -- lawyer turned inquiry agent, your most reliable interpreter of the visible world. His breathing is slightly elevated, his weight forward in the chair. The case troubles him more than he is saying. It troubles you too."
/117 "A large man -- his voice comes from above your own height and his footsteps on the kitchen floor are heavy and deliberate. His hands are calloused in the way of men who handle iron levers daily. His breathing is rapid but not evasive -- the breathing of grief and fear, not guilt."
/118 "Iver Mead, railway guard. His breathing is shallow and controlled -- the deliberate calm of a man who has been practising it. His weight is slightly forward on his feet. The smell of machine oil is present on his hands even through the soap."
/119 "Harold Pendleton, stockbroker. A large man -- his movements displace the air in the room when he shifts his weight. His cologne is expensive and generously applied. Beneath it: fear-sweat, faint but unmistakeable, present since before you arrived."
/120 "A heavy bench, immovable. The wood surface is a record of years of work: burns, gouges, chemical stains, the smoothed channels where tools have been laid repeatedly in the same positions. Your hands move slowly across it, reading its history. There is something here."
/121 "You touch each tool in turn. Six chisels, graduated in size, edges recently sharpened. Two hammers, a heavy and a light. Four files, their surfaces rough and purposeful. Everything hung in a specific order. The arrangement of a man who knows where each thing is without needing to search for it."
/122 "Mrs Hutchins. A faint draught from the direction of the window tells you where she stands, and her breathing -- quiet, controlled, effortful in its control -- tells you who she is. She believes her husband. She has decided not to break down in front of strangers."
/123 "Carlyle gives you a blank look. 'What stocks, Max? We have not yet established a financial motive. Perhaps we should read the Herald first.'"
/124 "You cannot find any way to open it. You need to examine it more carefully first."
/125 "You cannot locate a catch. Run your hands along the workbench first."
/126 "Air currents stir the hanging tools, making them chime. You count by sound alone: six chisels, two hammers, four files. 6-2-4. The numbers are deliberate."
/127 "The leather apron is stiff with dried compounds. Machine oil first, then something sharper -- chemical, precise. And beneath that, almost gone but not quite: the residue of an expensive cologne. You have encountered that cologne before, on a man whose fear-sweat it failed to mask."
/128 "The air carries machine oil and metal dust and something sharper beneath them -- precise and chemical. And there -- faint, almost gone -- the ghost of expensive cologne."
/129 "You have already retrieved the certificate from the desk. It is safe in your pocket."
/130 "Hutchins looks puzzled. Perhaps there is someone else here who knows something."
/131 "A section of the bench front that is not quite right -- the wood slightly proud of the surface around it, the grain interrupted. A drawer, concealed rather than locked, built to be missed rather than found. It has no handle you can detect."
/132 "A small wooden catch beneath the lip of the bench, painted over but not flush with the surrounding wood. It moves under your thumb with a small, precise resistance -- and then gives."

