/LTX

/0 "     The Eyes of Max Carrados#n     Based on Ernest Bramah#n     London, 1914#n#n     A DAAD Adventure#n#n"
/1 "The leather of your armchair is warm beneath your hands -- shaped precisely to you after forty years of use. The room comes to you through its signature: old books, beeswax polish, the cooling residue of last night's fire. The electric lamps are running at full power; you feel their warmth on your face as always. Carlyle's breathing tells you he is agitated. He has been turning the same page for ten minutes."
/2 "The winter air cuts sharply after the warmth of the study. Your feet find the familiar texture of the front steps -- three down, then the gravel path, then the pavement. The city announces itself: horses, wheels on cobbles, a steam whistle somewhere to the south. Parkinson is already at the motor car; you can hear the soft creak of leather as he straightens to attention."
/3 "The Dorvelle closes around you like a familiar glove. The leather seat, the smell of polished wood and motor oil, the particular sound the door makes when Carlyle pulls it shut behind him. Through the partition, Parkinson shifts in the driver's seat, awaiting your instructions. The city noise muffles to a distant hum."
/4 "Coal smoke is the first thing -- thick and domestic, the smell of a street where every chimney works hard against the cold. The pavement beneath your feet is uneven, worn down by decades of heavy boots. Children's voices carry from a yard nearby. Someone is frying onions. Number 107 is before you -- you can hear movement inside the house."
/5 "The motor car sits at the kerb. The neighbourhood comes to you through its sounds: a woman calling from somewhere above, the slow clop of a milk horse passing, children's voices circling the car with curiosity. Parkinson says nothing to them. He never does."
/6 "A kitchen scrubbed within an inch of its life -- the smell of carbolic soap and coal smoke and something baking. The table is old but the surface is clean and smooth beneath your hands. A kettle mutters on the range. Someone has put out the good cups. Hutchins is standing -- you can tell by the elevation of his voice, the way his weight shifts constantly from foot to foot. A man who cannot bring himself to sit in his own home."
/7 "After the city and the industrial flatness of Woundy, the air here is almost startling -- green and damp and alive. Birdsong. Real birdsong, not the urban sparrow variety but something more unhurried. The pavement gives way to packed earth under your feet. The neighbourhood carries wood smoke and wet leaves. Mead's cottage is here. You stand on the path for a moment, listening. The cottage is very quiet."
/8 "Parkinson has parked beneath what must be a large tree -- you can hear the branches moving overhead and feel the occasional drop of moisture on the roof. The contrast with the city is complete. Carlyle is quiet beside you, which means he is thinking. You are thinking too."
/9 "The cottage carries the particular smell of anxious tidiness -- fresh soap overlaying everything, applied this morning more thoroughly than habit requires. A clock ticks on the mantel with mechanical precision. Mead is to your right -- you can tell by the direction of his breathing, shallow and controlled, and by the slight displacement of air when he shifts his weight. A man who has rehearsed this conversation many times."
/10 "Without Carlyle, the room takes on a different quality. The working silence of a space that is usually occupied. The air carries machine oil and metal dust and something sharper beneath them -- precise and chemical. Tools clink softly on a rack to your right, still settling after the disturbance of the door. The floor changes texture under your feet: stone flags first, then the gritty resistance of metal filings, then fine wood shavings near the bench ahead."
/11 "The cold hits you as you step onto the platform -- a different kind of cold than the city, rawer and damp. The rails hum faintly with the vibration of a distant train running somewhere on the network. Creosote and engine smoke have soaked into the very wood beneath your feet. The signal box is east of here. You have been in a hundred signal boxes. You know exactly what to search for in this one."
/12 "Your motor car in the station yard at Woundy. The smell of railway -- coal smoke, hot metal, creosote -- is thick in the air. Somewhere beyond the yard, a goods train moves slowly, its rhythm felt as much as heard through the ground. Parkinson keeps the engine idling against the cold."
/13 "The box is empty -- the inquiry has suspended normal operations here. The equipment fills the small room with its own music: the tick and click of the block instruments, the occasional mechanical sigh of the lever frame. The levers themselves are cold iron under your hands, smooth with years of handling. The smell of oil and metal is thick. And something else -- a faint trace of something chemical, not quite right, beneath the familiar smell of the place."
/14 "The City comes at you from every direction -- rapid footsteps on stone, the rustle of newspapers, fragments of urgent conversation about yields and margins. The smell of cigar smoke and printer's ink and horses and ambition. Pendleton's office is in the building before you -- you can feel the edge of the stone steps with your stick."
/15 "The City traffic surrounds you -- the particular mix of iron wheels on cobbles and rubber tyres and hooves that tells you this is the financial district. A newsboy's voice cuts through, announcing something about railway stocks and recovery. In your pocket: a letter, three receipts, a diagram."
/16 "The office announces itself before Pendleton speaks. The smell of burned paper beneath the cigar smoke -- he has been destroying documents. A cold draught enters from somewhere to your right, a window left open despite the weather to clear the smell of burning. His chair scrapes quickly when he hears you announced -- the movement of a startled man. His voice, when it comes, is too large for the room. You find a chair without being shown to one and sit down. You hear Pendleton register this and decide not to comment. Good. He is already making mistakes."

